# banned-strings.list
#
# Truth-contract banned phrases for VitalCV public-facing surfaces. Any
# match in a non-allowlisted file fails CI.
#
# Lines starting with `#` are comments. Blank lines are ignored. Every
# other line is treated as a case-insensitive extended regex passed
# verbatim to `grep -iE`.
#
# To add a phrase: add the regex. To allow a file to contain it (e.g.
# a test asserting its absence, the policy doc itself, this very list):
# update `ALLOWLIST_GLOBS` in scripts/check-banned-strings.sh.
#
# See apps/web/CLAUDE.md for the authoritative truth contract.

# Compliance / certification overclaim
HIPAA compliant
SOC ?2 certified
NCQA certified
certified compliant
guaranteed compliant

# Verification overclaim
automatically verified
instantly verified
\bfully verified\b
real-time verification
real-time check
guaranteed verification
final verification without review
source confirmed before response

# Credentialing overclaim
\bcomplete credentialing\b
instant credentialing
eliminates credentialing
replaces credentialing

# Risk / acceptance overclaim
legally accepted
risk transferred
accepted everywhere
single source accepted by all hospitals

# Outcome overclaim (vague benefits attributed without evidence)
\balways up to date\b
solves burnout
solves diversity attrition

# === CASE-SENSITIVE SECTION ===
# Patterns below the marker are evaluated with `grep -E` (case-
# sensitive). The marker matters because the codebase uses the
# lowercase tokens `'verified'`, `'unavailable'`, etc. as INTERNAL
# state-machine enum values that the truth contract does NOT ban —
# the ban is for the CAPITALISED public-copy claim "Verified" used as
# a status label, not for canonical state names.
:case-sensitive:

# Bare "Verified" markers — match rendered JSX text and any bare
# quoted-string literal that is exactly "Verified" (single quotes,
# double quotes, or backticks). The scanner normalises smart quotes
# (U+2018, U+2019, U+201C, U+201D) to their ASCII counterparts BEFORE
# running grep, so the quote class here only needs ASCII glyphs.
#
# Mixed-case "Verified" only — lowercase `'verified'` (enum) and
# uppercase `'VERIFIED'` (canonical state constant) do not match. The
# all-caps form is reserved for internal canonical states; the public
# ban targets the buyer-facing capitalized label.
(label|status)\s*[:=]\s*['"`]Verified['"`]
['"`]Verified['"`]
>\s*Verified\s*<

# Compound overclaim phrases — "Verified provider" / "Verified source"
# are public-copy patterns that survive the bare-quote ban above
# because they carry a noun rather than a closing quote.
\bVerified provider\b
\bVerified source\b
